From openbsd-alpha  Tue Apr 02 23:28:59 2002
From: Ryan Anderson <ryan () nailedfrog ! com>
Date: Tue, 02 Apr 2002 23:28:59 +0000
To: openbsd-alpha
Subject: OACTIVE state on fxp nic causing firewall to die.
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-alpha&m=101779013109094

   This is kind of long winded, but I'm hoping someone here can help me
out. Please let me know if I should post this to the tech list.

I've built a nice bridging firewall on a Digital AlphaPC 164LX running
OpenBSD 3.0-current with pf. When we initially put it into production
however, it quickly started dropping all packets on the WAN interface
after a point, which caused soemthing of a fiasco... Since then I've had
it in a rigourous testing environment and have been attempted to
recreate the problems we saw in production, and I think I've finnally
nailed them down.

First off, my testing environment consists of a box to simulate the
external traffic (216.241.40.20) pluged into the alpha's WAN interface
(fxp1) via a crossover cable. The DMZ interface (fxp0) also has a single
box (216.241.43.8) plugged into it via a crossover cable, and the third
interface (which was going to be a nat'd lan) is not in use.

My first thought was that the wan interface started dropping connections
when the firewall had to keep track of too many stateful connections,
but after testing this proved not to be the case at all (indeed, the
firewall worked like a champ, even which pushed up to around 60000
concurrent connections), so next I decide to try testing if it had
difficulties with packetsize.

And here's where the errors start cropping up.

My first test to see how the firewall dealt with large packet size was a
simple ping. from the external box, I tried pinging with packetsizes
from 56-4048 bytes, and encountered no problems. However, when I tried
to do the same from the internal interface, disaster struck.
A simple ping from the DMZ box (216.241.43.8) of ping -s 2048 (and
larger) to the external WAN box (216.241.40.20) CRASHED THE KERNEL. ddb
came up indicating a panic had taken place, asking me to do a trace and
ps (you can find the output of those attached at the end). I discovered
this did not occur when I turned pf off.

Now, that seems pretty bad to me, but it still wasn't replicating the
error I got when the firwall went into production, because it hadn't
crashed then, it had just stopped forwarding packets. So next I tried
trasnfering large files.

I used scp for this test, and used files around 250megs. First I tried
transfering a 250meg file from the DMZ box to the WAN box, and it went
through okay, the second time I tried doing two scp sessions, both
sending the same file from the DMZ box to the WAN box at the same time,
and bingo, half way through both sessions stalled. looking at the
ifconfig on the openbsd box showed that fxp1 (the wan interface) had the
flag "OACTIVE" set, where it hadn't been previously, and now the
firewall was exhibiting exactly the same behavior as before when it was
in production, no packets were being trasmited accross the WAN interface.

tcpdump on fxp1 still showed packets being recieved, but then never came
through to the other side of the bridge. takeing fxp1 down and back up
with ifconfig cleared the flag, and normal activity resumed imediately.
I then tried going the opposite direction, from the WAN box to the DMZ
box, and the exact same thing occured, only this time the OACTIVE flag
was on DMZ interface, fxp0.

Taking down either of the interfaces in the bridge seems to clear
oactive, reguadless of whch interface the flag is actually set on. I
also tried using a dec tulip based nic (de driver, haven't tried dc) and
had the same thing happen. (though testing wasn't as extensive with this
card as with the fxp's) . Also having pf on or off doesn't seem to
affect this happening.

This OACTIVE flag seems fairly obscure. Websearches and searches of the
openbsd mailing list archives have turned up rather few refrences to it,
but it appears to me to be set when the outgoing buffer on the card is
full, or at least when the OS thinks that it is, and for some reason the
flag is not getting reset when the buffer empties.

Well, if anyone has any ideas or a patch that can fix this problem, I'd
love to hear it. I'm fairly convinced that the OACTIVE bug is related to
the bridge going on between the WAN and DMZ nics, and I've fairly sure
the crash bug is in pf somewhere and is probably only happening on my
alpha architechture since I imagine someone would have noticed it before
now if it was more widespread.

Thanks again for your time, Please let me know if I should post this
somewhere else, or if there's any other information that would be
helpful. attached below are my settings.

-Ryan Anderson
ryan@nailedfrog.com


